ID: B3-Ch02c-Assassinate
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_gontrok == 3) {
"Maybe somebody else is also trying to assassinate the king."

"That brings up a fair question," Hadrik says, wiping grit and dried blood from his beard. He squints out of his right eye, his left eyelid swollen completely shut and bruised a dark, mottled purple. "If the king is already a corpse by the time we corner him, does that void our oath with Melindra?"

"Dead or breathing, as long as you help pry loose what he took from me, the agreement stands," Melindra says, brushing stone dust from her sleeves. "You have my word on that."

"We cannot linger here," Daren says. He drags his sword up from the cracked flagstones, his scarred knuckles white on the grip as he leans heavily on the hilt before limping toward us. "The royal guards may be gone, but regular city patrols will flood this square any second. We need cover to rest and patch ourselves up."

"Brimstone and iron," Hadrik says, tilting his head to gauge Daren's ragged posture with his good eye. "What hit you? You look like a collapsed watchtower."

"Look who's talking," Daren mutters, halting beside us with a sharp wince as his cracked ribs hitch his breath. "Catch your reflection in a puddle sometime. Your cheek looks like a mashed turnip. I would weave a mend, but my reserves are empty. It took everything I had just to cast a recovery weave to stand upright."

"Shelter comes first," Kate says, her cloak pulled tight across her chest as her dark eyes sweep the silent plaza. "Standing in the open makes us targets. Finding a secure corner in unfamiliar enemy territory, however, is another matter."

"Perhaps we can offer some guidance," a man calls out.

He steps out from one of the narrow alleys branching off the square, flanked by two armed youths in their mid-twenties. He looks fifty-odd, gray-bearded, wearing an oil-stained white lab coat over working clothes and a calm, reassuring smile.

"And who might you be, if you don't mind me--" Daren cuts off as Leila darts past him, boots kicking up stone dust as she sprints straight for the newcomer.

"Father!" Leila's glowing blue letters flare wide in the air before she throws her arms around his neck.

"Leila, my dear girl... I feared the worst," the man says, pulling her tight against his coat.

"Father, these are my friends," Leila writes the moment she steps back, her hands flying in swift, precise arcs. "They broke me out of the arena cells and escorted me across the continent."

"I gathered as much," her father says, looking over at us with genuine relief. "I owe every one of you a debt for keeping my daughter safe, but proper introductions can wait until we are off these streets."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_gontrok != 3) {
"Maybe somebody else is also trying to assassinate the king."

"That brings up a fair question," Hadrik says, wiping grit and dried blood from his beard. He squints out of his right eye, his left eyelid swollen completely shut and bruised a dark, mottled purple. "If the king is already a corpse by the time we corner him, does that void our oath with Melindra?"

"Dead or breathing, as long as you help pry loose what he took from me, the agreement stands," Melindra says, brushing stone dust from her sleeves. "You have my word on that."

"We cannot linger here," Daren says. He limps toward us, bending stiffly to scoop his sword from where I dropped it when the captain had me pinned, his breath hissing through his teeth. "The royal guards may be gone, but regular city patrols will flood this square any second. We need cover to rest and patch ourselves up."

"Brimstone and iron," Hadrik says, tilting his head to gauge Daren's ragged posture with his good eye. "What hit you? You look like a collapsed watchtower."

"Look who's talking," Daren mutters, halting beside us with a sharp wince as his cracked ribs hitch his breath. "Catch your reflection in a puddle sometime. Your cheek looks like a mashed turnip. I would weave a mend, but my reserves are empty. It took everything I had just to cast a recovery weave to stand upright."

"Shelter comes first," Kate says, her cloak pulled tight across her chest as her dark eyes sweep the silent plaza. "Standing in the open makes us targets. Finding a secure corner in unfamiliar enemy territory, however, is another matter."

"Perhaps we can offer some guidance," a man calls out.

He steps out from one of the narrow alleys branching off the square, flanked by two armed youths in their mid-twenties. He looks fifty-odd, gray-bearded, wearing an oil-stained white lab coat over working clothes and a calm, reassuring smile.

"And who might you be, if you don't mind me--" Daren cuts off as Leila darts past him, boots kicking up stone dust as she sprints straight for the newcomer.

"Father!" Leila's glowing blue letters flare wide in the air before she throws her arms around his neck.

"Leila, my dear girl... I feared the worst," the man says, pulling her tight against his coat.

"Father, these are my friends," Leila writes the moment she steps back, her hands flying in swift, precise arcs. "They broke me out of the arena cells and escorted me across the continent."

"I gathered as much," her father says, looking over at us with genuine relief. "I owe every one of you a debt for keeping my daughter safe, but proper introductions can wait until we are off these streets."

}
"Am I right in assuming you have a route out of here?" Kate asks.

"Indeed," Leila's father says. "Follow closely, please."

We trade quick nods and slip after Leila, her father, and the two guards into the shadows of the alley. As we hustle between high stone walls, her father fishes a transceiver from his coat pocket and speaks into the mesh.

"Barry, do you copy?"

"Not another one," I groan. Hadrik snorts, and a cackle ripples from Arraka's amulet.

"Barry!" Leila's father raises his voice over the laughter.

"Give me a moment," he tells us over his shoulder. "The seredium plating in the bedrock plays havoc with the signal... Is something the matter?"

"Pay us no mind," Hadrik says between chuckles. "Our scrawny friend here shares the name, and this is far from the first time we've tripped over another Barry on the road."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Transceiver, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Transceiver)


