choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Transceiver, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Transceiver)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Transceiver
TEXT:

"Ah!" Leila's father chuckles. "I see now. Pay them no heed, my friend. I happen to think 'Barry' is a fine name."

"Hey, much obliged, Doc!" the transceiver squawks back. "What brought that on out of nowhere?"

"I was not addressing you," Leila's father mutters into the casing. "I was speaking to another Barry who-- never mind that. Can you lock onto our position? Is the signal clear enough for a transport?"

"Loud and clear," the voice replies. "Ready when you are. Shall I pull you down?"

"Immediately," Leila's father says. "Take the entire group. Leave no one behind."

"Understood. Bracing in three, two..."

The alley dissolves in a flash of blinding white light. Weight drops out from under my boots, my stomach lurches into my throat, and the sudden pop of shifting air pressure leaves my ears ringing. When the glare fades, the alley walls are gone.

We stand in a cavernous stone chamber, subterranean chill prickling our skin. The only illumination comes from jagged clusters of pale blue crystals embedded throughout the dark, metallic stone of the walls and high ceiling--pure seredium, by the dense, inert pull of the rock. Strange, pale underground ferns and thick-stemmed mosses sprout from crevices in the stone, fed by the faint crystal glow.

Ahead, behind a wide metal console covered in toggles, switches, and inscribed dials, stands a burly man in work clothes. Behind him looms a massive metallic contraption, humming with stored power and blinking with dozens of colored indicator lights. I have heard accounts of the ancient Lessathi teleportation arrays that hauled tournament contestants across oceans to Varathia, but this is the first time I have seen one functioning at arm's length.

"Thank you for the pull, Barry," Leila's father says, stepping past the platform.

"Any time, Doc," the man behind the desk says, wiping grease on his trousers. "Who are the guests? Friends of yours?"

"This is my daughter, Leila," her father says with quiet pride, gesturing toward her. "The girl I have spoken of so often. And these are her companions, who saw her safely to Ollendor. Regrettably, I have not yet had the chance to learn their names. Leila, would you do the honors?"

Leila offers a quick nod. In rapid succession, glowing blue script ignites in the air above each of our heads, crisp and clear in the gloom.

"A pleasure to meet you all," Leila's father says, offering a slight bow. "I am Reidzek. My companions here are Peter and Albert, and over at the controls is Barry, as you have already heard."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Homeland, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Homeland)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Homeland
TEXT:

"Ah, now I follow," the operator says, reading the floating letters above my brow. "You were talking to this Barry on the horn earlier. Takes me back to growing up with two other Barrys on the same block. We had to pass out handles just to keep from answering each other's mothers."

"Same trick where I hail from," Hadrik says, grinning broadly. "Whenever another Hadrik showed up in the tavern, the lads just called me 'dwarf'. We ought to pick a proper title for our Barry here. Barry the Improviser. Barry the Reckless."

"Plain 'Barry' suits me fine, thanks," I say.

"Come now, Reckless," Hadrik says, giving me a solid elbow in the ribs. "We can find something grand. Or tack your father's name on the end, the way highborn lords on the Western Continent drag their family trees behind them to sound imposing. What do you reckon?"

"No need to saddle the lad with a title if he dislikes it," the operator says. "I already carry an old handle from my youth. If it saves us all a headache, feel free to call me Thundercloud."

"Thundercloud has a fine ring to it," Hadrik says. "Right then, Thundercloud... mind giving us the lay of the land? Are we beneath the city streets, or somewhere else entirely?"

"Under the city, I believe," Thundercloud says, scratching the back of his neck. "They covered the geography during orientation, but that was years ago. Doctor, your briefing was recent. Is the platform beneath Ollendor?"

"Directly below the city center, several hundred paces deep," Reidzek explains. "The ceiling above us is solid bedrock layered with seredium. As far as official routes go, this teleporter is the only viable path between here and the surface. The tunnels branch out for leagues in every direction, so an unmapped natural exit might exist somewhere in the deep dark, but navigating that maze without a guide guarantees starvation long before you find sunlight."

"How did you locate us?" Leila's blue script hangs before her father. "Were you patrolling the market, or did someone alert you to our arrival?"

"We trailed you from the south gate," says Albert, the taller of the two bodyguards. "Peter and I, that is, not Doctor Reidzek. The doctor mentioned his daughter had silver hair, so the moment we spotted your group, we signaled Thundercloud to brief the doctor and beam him up. When you clashed with the royal guards, we had to scramble. We convinced the king of the underground to dispatch a team and detonate a distraction at the temple plaza, hoping the commotion would draw the captain away from you."

"Wait," Daren says, his brow furrowing. "You were the ones responsible for that blast at the ceremony?"

"Our operatives on the surface, yes," Albert says. "The mad king invariably recalls his personal guard whenever he smells danger, so a well-placed charge was the fastest way to rattle his cage."

choice(""What happened to your friends that set off the explosion? Are they all safe?"", B3-Ch02c-Safe, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Safe)
choice(""How did they manage to pull it off? Didn't they have people guarding that place?"", B3-Ch02c-Guarding, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Guarding)
choice(""Wasn't this a little risky, though? Why did your friends agree to do it just for us?"", B3-Ch02c-Risky, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Risky)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Safe
TEXT:

"What happened to your friends that set off the explosion? Are they all safe?"

"We confirmed their retreat before stepping into your alley," says Peter, the second guard. "They slipped clean away and will rendezvous down here once their surface tasks are wrapped up."

"You mentioned a 'king of the underground' earlier," Illuna says, her eyes narrowing as she fixes Albert with a cool gaze. "Who is this individual?"

"The patron and protector of our subterranean sanctuary," Albert says. "He provides us with shelter, rations, and advanced equipment to aid our struggle against the tyrant above. In return for his backing, we agreed to launch no major operations without his explicit sanction, and to undertake occasional private tasks on his behalf, even when they diverge from our main objective."

"So you are staging a rebellion," Hadrik says.

"A targeted resistance," Albert corrects. "We have no desire to plunge Ollendor into civil war or drown the streets in the blood of our kin. Our fight is strictly with the corrupt crown that has bled this kingdom dry. When word reached our benefactor that you were holding your ground against the royal guard, he took an immediate interest. He authorized the distraction and requested an audience, hoping to recruit your blades or forge a pact. We should not idle here, however. Our compound lies further down the passage. Follow us to the antechamber."

"Until later, Barry," Reidzek calls out as we file toward the corridor. "Ah, apologies--Thundercloud. That will take practice."

"Take care, Doc," Thundercloud waves from his console. "Good to see your girl back in one piece."

"Father, why do they refer to you as 'doctor'?" Leila writes, stepping alongside him. "Have you taken up medicine in my absence?"

"Mainly the coat, I suspect," Reidzek says, tugging at his white lapel with a small chuckle. "I wear it in the workshop to keep grease and machine oil off my tunic. It happens to resemble physician garb, and the locals needed a title since my real name trips their tongues."

"It is more than the coat, Doctor," Peter speaks up. "On our first encounter, the city watch nearly hauled you off because you were binding the wounds of a rebel operative bleeding out in the gutter. Without your field surgery, he would have died before we reached him. You have also restored half the enchanted medical devices in our infirmary, saving dozens of our people. We owe you a tremendous debt."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Seredium, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Seredium)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Guarding
TEXT:

"How did they manage to pull it off? Didn't they have people guarding that place?"

"Those charges were wired into the foundations months ago as a contingency," says Peter, the second guard. "A distraction in reserve. Our runners only needed to get close enough to trigger the ignition runes, not fight past the perimeter guards."

"You mentioned a 'king of the underground' earlier," Illuna says, her eyes narrowing as she fixes Albert with a cool gaze. "Who is this individual?"

"The patron and protector of our subterranean sanctuary," Albert says. "He provides us with shelter, rations, and advanced equipment to aid our struggle against the tyrant above. In return for his backing, we agreed to launch no major operations without his explicit sanction, and to undertake occasional private tasks on his behalf, even when they diverge from our main objective."

