"It is more than the coat, Doctor," Peter speaks up. "On our first encounter, the city watch nearly hauled you off because you were binding the wounds of a rebel operative bleeding out in the gutter. Without your field surgery, he would have died before we reached him. You have also restored half the enchanted medical devices in our infirmary, saving dozens of our people. We owe you a tremendous debt."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Seredium, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Seredium)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Risky
TEXT:

"Wasn't this a little risky, though? Why did your friends agree to do it just for us?"

"Far less dangerous than storming the temple steps," says Peter, the second guard. "No one engaged the garrison. They primed the fuse from the crowd and vanished into the side streets before the smoke even cleared."

"You mentioned a 'king of the underground' earlier," Illuna says, her eyes narrowing as she fixes Albert with a cool gaze. "Who is this individual?"

"The patron and protector of our subterranean sanctuary," Albert says. "He provides us with shelter, rations, and advanced equipment to aid our struggle against the tyrant above. In return for his backing, we agreed to launch no major operations without his explicit sanction, and to undertake occasional private tasks on his behalf, even when they diverge from our main objective."

"So you are staging a rebellion," Hadrik says.

"A targeted resistance," Albert corrects. "We have no desire to plunge Ollendor into civil war or drown the streets in the blood of our kin. Our fight is strictly with the corrupt crown that has bled this kingdom dry. When word reached our benefactor that you were holding your ground against the royal guard, he took an immediate interest. He authorized the distraction and requested an audience, hoping to recruit your blades or forge a pact. We should not idle here, however. Our compound lies further down the passage. Follow us to the antechamber."

"Until later, Barry," Reidzek calls out as we file toward the corridor. "Ah, apologies--Thundercloud. That will take practice."

"Take care, Doc," Thundercloud waves from his console. "Good to see your girl back in one piece."

"Father, why do they refer to you as 'doctor'?" Leila writes, stepping alongside him. "Have you taken up medicine in my absence?"

"Mainly the coat, I suspect," Reidzek says, tugging at his white lapel with a small chuckle. "I wear it in the workshop to keep grease and machine oil off my tunic. It happens to resemble physician garb, and the locals needed a title since my real name trips their tongues."

"It is more than the coat, Doctor," Peter speaks up. "On our first encounter, the city watch nearly hauled you off because you were binding the wounds of a rebel operative bleeding out in the gutter. Without your field surgery, he would have died before we reached him. You have also restored half the enchanted medical devices in our infirmary, saving dozens of our people. We owe you a tremendous debt."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Seredium, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Seredium)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Seredium
TEXT:

"No thanks are necessary, Peter," Reidzek says quietly. "I am the one indebted to your people for shelter when I had nowhere left to run. I only pray our labor bears fruit. My sole wish is to walk the surface with my daughter in open daylight, without glancing over our shoulders at every shadow."

"The tyrant holds that tight a grip, does he?" Hadrik asks.

"Tighter," Albert says. "The king rules on whim. Look crossways at a patrolman in the morning, and by nightfall your house stands empty, your name scrubbed from the ledgers. We count ourselves fortunate to have carved out this sanctuary in the deep caverns, but the tunnels cannot house a city's worth of refugees. Not to mention the air--most of our oxygen comes from those pale ferns clinging to the rock, and their yield has strict limits."

"I have never encountered vegetation like this," Daren says, limping closely at our heels. "What is the species?"

"They have no formal classification," Reidzek says, gesturing toward a cluster of pale fronds whose fibrous roots grip the metallic seams of the rock face. "They appear to subsist entirely on trace minerals leached from the seredium and the faint radiation of the blue crystals. The root systems must be remarkably dense to penetrate rock that dense."

"Which reminds me," Albert says, turning around as we walk. "The seredium density in these shafts smothers magical communication. If you ever need to broadcast to the surface through a transceiver, you must do it from Thundercloud's platform. That chamber sits directly beneath a natural fault where the seredium layer thins enough for the signal to pierce the crust."

"And that platform..." I say, glancing back down the corridor. "Is that the same class of teleporter that brought tournament contestants to Varathia from across the sea?"

"Far from it," Albert says, shaking his head. "That rig barely covers Ollendor's outer perimeter, and only because it sits dead center beneath the city. True intercontinental transport arrays are ancient, gargantuan constructs, heavily fortified and locked away by the continent's rulers. You will find nothing of that magnitude in Ollendor."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Banshees, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Banshees)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Banshees
TEXT:

"Pardon the intrusion," Peter says, glancing hesitantly toward Kate and Illuna as we move through the corridor. "I watched you two fighting on the plaza. Tell me... are you both banshees?"

"Only I am a banshee," Illuna replies, her tone flat and measured. "Kate is an artificial mage. She was not born with her link."

"Ah..." Peter blinks, caught off balance. "I understand."

"Furthermore, I share this physical vessel with a child named Flower, who commands fire," Illuna adds. "The flame jets you observed in the square were hers, not mine."

"You share a body?" Peter's eyes widen. "With your host?"

"Ah!" Albert snaps his fingers. "That explains why Doctor Reidzek's daughter conjured two names over your head during the introductions. So you are Illuna?"

"I am," Illuna says.

"Banshee, fire child, or tournament mage, our king will welcome the steel," Albert says.

"When you say king, you mean your underground patron, right?" Hadrik grumbles. "Because the royal titles down here are getting tangled."

"Our patron, yes," Albert says, gesturing ahead as the tunnel widens into a vast, high-ceilinged hall. A dozen sturdy wooden chairs line the entry wall, and several armed men and women converse in small knots near the center. "This is the antechamber. Rest your feet. Peter and I will inform the king of your arrival."

As the guards turn away, a man in his forties detaches himself from a far table and saunters toward us. He has a rugged build, weathered features, and a thick scar cleaving across his left eye. A broadsword hangs at his hip over boiled leather armor, and his right hand loosely grips an iron-shod casting staff. The sharp, volatile hum of his aura sets my stat booster buzzing with a warning sigil.

"Ho, Albert," the scarred man calls out, his gaze raking across our battered group. "Are these the outsiders Barry hailed on the horn?"

"The very same," Albert says.

"Well now," the man whistles softly, looking us up and down. "Walked away from the royal guard in one piece? Not bad. Not bad in the slightest."

"They engaged only three," Albert notes. "The captain, the mercenary, and the shapeshifter. The rest of the guard was stationed elsewhere."

"You took on the captain?" The scarred man raises a brow, looking genuinely taken aback. "That explains the bruised ribs and torn cloaks. What brand of lunacy drives someone to smuggle themselves into the most heavily fortified city on the continent?"

choice(""It's because we heard that you guys are serving some really good bagels around here."", B3-Ch02c-Artificial, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Artificial, v_b3_ch2_madness = 0)
choice(""We only came here to do some trading. It's all just one big misunderstanding."", B3-Ch02c-Artificial, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Artificial, v_b3_ch2_madness = 1)
choice(""We actually came here to kill the king of Ollendor."", B3-Ch02c-Artificial, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Artificial, v_b3_ch2_madness = 2)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Artificial
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_madness == 0) {
"It's because we heard that you guys are serving some really good bagels around here."

"If that brought you across the border, prepare for disappointment," the scarred man says. "The bakeries down here produce leaden lumps of dough. Don't let anyone sell you on the crust."

"A tragedy," I say. "But since we're already here, we might as well take in the sights. It would be a waste of a trip otherwise."

"Right," he says. "Tell me, outsiders... since you've tangled with the royal guard, did you run into the Overseer and his droons as well?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_madness == 1) {
"We only came here to do some trading. It's all just one big misunderstanding."

"Naturally," the scarred man says with a dry smirk. "Who wouldn't sign their life away in a blood tournament just to peddle wares in a kingdom they've never set foot in?"

"Precisely," I say. "Glad we see eye to eye."

"Tell me, 'merchants'... since you've tangled with the royal guard, did you run into the Overseer and his droons as well?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_madness == 2) {
"We actually came here to kill the king of Ollendor."

"Ambitious," the scarred man says, letting out a low whistle. "Though hardly original. You aren't the first blades-for-hire to march in with regal ambitions. The previous attempts decorate the castle dungeons, in case that wasn't obvious."

"We manage," I say.

"Evidently," he says, taking in Daren's limp and Hadrik's swollen jaw. "Tell me, outsiders... since you've tangled with the royal guard, did you run into the Overseer and his droons as well?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4 || v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3) {
"The Overseer?" I say. "You mean that green-eyed floating orb that talks like a ledger? Yeah, we met."

"You think he's just a chatterbox piece of scrap, but the bastard commands every red-eyed drone patrolling the skies," the man says. "He can direct elemental barrages or monitor every alley in Ollendor. Most folks here take him lightly, but in my book, that machine is deadlier than Billy Bob and the royal guard combined."

"You insist on calling it a 'he', but the Overseer is an 'it'," Albert cuts in. "An automated control matrix built by ancient Lessathi, patched together by Billy Bob. Artificial reasoning does not equate to personhood."

"You say that because you've never looked it in the lens!" the scarred man snaps. "It's putting on an act. It sounds clunky and literal, but mark my words--one morning that machine will unseat the king and claim Ollendor for itself. Don't claim I didn't warn you when the sky turns green."

"Yes, Breynan," Albert sighs. "You have warned us a thousand times. Perhaps we can table the mechanical apocalypse and attend to present business?"

"Hold on," Hadrik says, scratching his beard. "You're telling me that green-eyed scrap of metal is actually alive?"

Albert rubs his temples.

"Not alive by any natural standard," he says. "Merely sophisticated enough to weigh variables and execute decisions."

"Is that even possible?" Hadrik asks. "Stitching intelligence into an object by tuning its aura? Daren, you work metal. Have you ever heard of such a thing?"

"I didn't even realize inanimate objects possessed distinct auras until Arraka pointed it out recently," Daren says. "I always assumed my weaves interacted with the ambient mana clinging to the surface. Object auras behave so differently from living souls that I wouldn't know where the limits lie."

"Of course it is possible, you dullards!" Arraka's voice shrills from the amulet. "It is the same underlying principle as construct animating. If a stone golem can track intruders and crush skulls without a handler, why not an Overseer? This grand 'artificial intelligence' is nothing more than a glorified golem core with an expanded instruction set. Treating it as a divine miracle only exposes your ignorance."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins != 3 && v_b3_ch2_goblins != 4 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 3) {
"I'm not sure who this Overseer is supposed to be, but if by 'drowns' you mean those flying metal balls, then yeah, we did meet them," I say. "Those pieces of junk nearly killed us."

"Aye," the scarred man says. "The droons are lethal enough on their own, but the Overseer is the real threat. He commands the entire fleet and feeds on their reconnaissance. Most folks here take him lightly, but in my book, that machine is deadlier than Billy Bob and the royal guard combined."

"You insist on calling it a 'he', but the Overseer is an 'it'," Albert cuts in. "An automated control matrix built by ancient Lessathi, patched together by Billy Bob. Artificial reasoning does not equate to personhood."

"You say that because you've never looked it in the lens!" the scarred man snaps. "It's putting on an act. It sounds clunky and literal, but mark my words--one morning that machine will unseat the king and claim Ollendor for itself. Don't claim I didn't warn you when the sky turns green."

"Yes, Breynan," Albert sighs. "You have warned us a thousand times. Perhaps we can table the mechanical apocalypse and attend to present business?"

"Hold on," Hadrik says, scratching his beard. "You're saying the ancient Lessathi found a way to breathe life into dead metal?"

Albert rubs his temples.

"The Overseer is not alive by any natural standard," he says. "Merely sophisticated enough to weigh variables and execute decisions."

"Is that even possible?" Hadrik asks. "Stitching intelligence into an object by tuning its aura? Daren, you work metal. Have you ever heard of such a thing?"

"I didn't even realize inanimate objects possessed distinct auras until Arraka pointed it out recently," Daren says. "I always assumed my weaves interacted with the ambient mana clinging to the surface. Object auras behave so differently from living souls that I wouldn't know where the limits lie."

"Of course it is possible, you dullards!" Arraka's voice shrills from the amulet. "It is the same underlying principle as construct animating. If a stone golem can track intruders and crush skulls without a handler, why not an Overseer? This grand 'artificial intelligence' is nothing more than a glorified golem core with an expanded instruction set. Treating it as a divine miracle only exposes your ignorance."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Alice, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Alice)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Alice
TEXT:

"So in theory, if I tuned the auras of a few boulders, I could raise a private army of tactical stone golems?" Hadrik asks, his eyes lighting up.

"Certainly," Arraka sneers. "If you fuse those boulders with refined seredium, resonant blue crystals, a dozen catalyst alloys, and spend forty years studying core matrices from the Magium. Check back with me in three decades and let me know how your pebbles are doing."

"In any case," Albert says, "the crucial detail is that the Overseer remains at odds with Billy Bob. That friction is why the Lessathi has withheld full administrative clearance over the city's aerial fleet, keeping the construct classified as 'experimental.' We suspect the Overseer pursues its own objectives, though its endgame remains opaque. Winning its allegiance would provide immense leverage--those airborne units are formidable."

"Why do you keep butchering the pronunciation?" Melindra asks, crossing her arms. "They are 'droons.' That is how Billy Bob refers to them."

"I could swear I heard him say 'drowns,'" Albert mutters. "Or 'droynes.' Regardless, the label is absurd. Let us call them flying spheres and move on."

"I prefer 'golem heads,'" Melindra says. "On account of the glowing central eye."

"Whatever suits you," Albert says with a weary wave. "Come, Peter, we have kept our patron waiting long enough."

"Excuse me..." a small voice pipe up from beside the entry bench. A girl of perhaps seven years steps forward, wringing the hem of a faded smock. "Did you come from the surface? Are you real warriors?"

"We are," Daren says gently, taking a half-step toward her. "What troubles you, child?"

"Please, sirs..." Her small hands reach out, clutching weakly at my sleeve and Daren's gauntlet. "Could you find my family? My mother was taken three days ago... My father and brother even longer. These people promised to look for them, but they lied! They haven't searched at all. Please... You are my only hope."

"Come now, Alice," Albert says, catching the girl by the shoulder and pulling her back with firm gentleness. "You know better than to pester guests."

"Yes, sir..." Alice's voice drops to a whisper, her head sinking toward her chest. "I'm sorry. I won't do it again..."

"Our apologies," Albert tells us, motioning toward the inner archway. "Let us go, Peter."

"It is... no trouble at all," Daren murmurs, his gaze lingering on the child as the two men lead her down the corridor.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Alice2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Alice2)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Alice2
TEXT:

"What was that about?" Hadrik asks Breynan in a low rumble.

"The girl?" Breynan says, his expression hardening. "Our scouts brought her in during a sweep. The city watch cleared out her entire household. Her father and brother were registered mages who vanished from their beds one night without a trace. They aren't isolated cases. Unexplained abductions have escalated sharply over the past few years, and citizens are terrified to whisper about it, fearing they'll be dragged into the dark next."

"Do you know why mages are being targeted?" Daren asks, watching Albert and Peter pause mid-hall to speak with another rebel while Alice stands with bowed head.

"Given the pattern, we suspect they are funneled into the black seredium towers," Breynan says. "Those monoliths power every Lessathi contraption in Ollendor--the floating carriages, the sky conduits, the aerial fleet. The grid runs on mana siphoned from spellcasters stationed on the external gantry platforms and deep within the inner chambers. It began as paid contracting, but Billy Bob evidently found voluntary labor insufficient and resorted to forced conscription. It remains an educated guess, of course."

"Have your scouts infiltrated the towers to confirm if the girl's kin are inside?" Daren presses.

"The perimeter is impenetrable without heavy casualties," Breynan says bluntly. "We cannot risk dozens of lives on a reconnaissance hunch, and staging a breakout would require a full regiment. Harsh as it sounds, our few blades cannot be spent chasing a single family."

"And her mother?" Hadrik asks. "What became of her?"

"She disappeared shortly after filing inquiries with the watch commander," Breynan says. "Pushed too hard and annoyed the wrong lieutenant. Only the gods know where she ended up... assuming she still draws breath."

Across the hall, Albert and Peter conclude their exchange and guide Alice toward the far archway.

choice("Call after the girl and tell her that we'll find her family.", B3-Ch02c-Sundown, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Sundown, v_b3_ch2_alice = 1)
choice("Stay silent.", B3-Ch02c-Sundown, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Sundown, v_b3_ch2_alice = 0)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Sundown
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_alice == 0) {
Daren and Hadrik continue conferring with Breynan as the girl disappears down the corridor with Albert and Peter. Resistance members drift through the hall on routine errands, but the quiet routine shatters a few minutes later.

Near the corridor entrance, two young fighters drag an older man toward a reinforced iron door set into the bedrock. The slab is reinforced with heavy crossbars and half a dozen iron padlocks, beneath a faded stencil reading 'Quarantine' in chipped red paint.

"Another intake for the ward, Desmond," one of the youths calls out to the sentry. "Unbar the hatch."

"Right," Desmond grunts, selecting a heavy iron key from his ring and working the locks one by one.

"Please..." the older man begs, digging his heels into the flagstones. "I still have two hours before the mark turns hot. At least let me say goodbye to my boy!"

"Your boy is on patrol outside the perimeter, Jack," the youth says, keeping a tight grip on his collar. "He won't be back until sundown. You know the rules."

"Two hours!" Jack pleads. "You cannot lock me in the dark like a dog!"

"Do not make this harder, Jack," the other youth says, his tone weary rather than cruel. "Your run is finished. You know what happens when the fever breaks. What do two hours buy except more grief? Keep your feet. None of us enjoy this."

"Clear the threshold," Desmond says, swinging the heavy door inward.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_alice == 1) {
"Hey, Alice!" I shout across the hall. "Don't worry, we'll find your family for you!"

The girl stops in her tracks, whirling around with a fragile spark igniting in her tired eyes.

"You mean that?" she calls back, her voice shaking.

"Count on it," I say firmly. "We'll track them down and bring them back."

A breathless gasp escapes her, and tears spill over her cheeks.

"Thank you!" she cries, clutching her smock with trembling hands. "Thank you so much! I'll earn it, I swear! I can wash your dishes... I'll cook your rations... scrub the floors, carry gear, anything--"

"Save the chores," I tell her with a brief wave. "Just get some sleep. You've earned a rest."

She wipes her eyes with the back of her sleeve, offering a faint, trembling smile before turning to follow Albert and Peter into the inner passage.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_alice == 1 && v_b3_ch2_madness == 2) {
"Barry, I understand your instinct, but tossing out promises like that is dangerous," Daren says in an undertone. "We have no lead on her kin, no guarantee they survive, and mounting a rescue pulls us completely off course from why we entered this city."

"Does it?" I ask. "Once the king falls, the garrison collapses into chaos, leaving the seredium towers wide open. And when we install someone reasonable on the throne, a single decree forces the guard captains to cough up where they dumped the mother. Chasing her family fits right into the job."

"You're building castles on sand, Barry," Daren mutters, though the edge leaves his voice. "Still... even an indirect strike does more for that child than the resistance has managed. We will see how the pieces land."

Near the corridor entrance, two young fighters drag an older man toward a reinforced iron door set into the bedrock. The slab is reinforced with heavy crossbars and half a dozen iron padlocks, beneath a faded stencil reading 'Quarantine' in chipped red paint.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_alice == 1 && v_b3_ch2_madness != 2) {
"Barry, I understand your instinct, but tossing out promises like that is dangerous," Daren says in an undertone. "We have no lead on her kin, no guarantee they survive, and mounting a rescue pulls us completely off course from why we entered this city."

"Does it?" I ask. "Once the king falls, the garrison collapses into chaos, leaving the seredium towers wide open. And when we install someone reasonable on the throne, a single decree forces the guard captains to cough up where they dumped the mother. Chasing her family fits right into the job."

"You're building castles on sand, Barry," Daren mutters, though the edge leaves his voice. "Still... even an indirect strike does more for that child than the resistance has managed. We will see how the pieces land."

"Well now," Breynan mutters, catching my eye with a sharp grin. "So that's your game. Stalking the crown itself... The patron will be thrilled to hear that."

Near the corridor entrance, two young fighters drag an older man toward a reinforced iron door set into the bedrock. The slab is reinforced with heavy crossbars and half a dozen iron padlocks, beneath a faded stencil reading 'Quarantine' in chipped red paint.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_alice == 1) {
"Another intake for the ward, Desmond," one of the youths calls out to the sentry. "Unbar the hatch."

"Right," Desmond grunts, selecting a heavy iron key from his ring and working the locks one by one.

"Please..." the older man begs, digging his heels into the flagstones. "I still have two hours before the mark turns hot. At least let me say goodbye to my boy!"

"Your boy is on patrol outside the perimeter, Jack," the youth says, keeping a tight grip on his collar. "He won't be back until sundown. You know the rules."

"Two hours!" Jack pleads. "You cannot lock me in the dark like a dog!"

"Do not make this harder, Jack," the other youth says, his tone weary rather than cruel. "Your run is finished. You know what happens when the fever breaks. What do two hours buy except more grief? Keep your feet. None of us enjoy this."

"Clear the threshold," Desmond says, swinging the heavy door inward.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Quarantine, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Quarantine)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Quarantine
TEXT:

"No! No, please!" Jack yells as the two youths heave him past the threshold.

Desmond slams the heavy iron door shut and throws the deadbolts, the metal clanging loud in the chamber.

"Let me out!" Jack's muffled fists hammer frantically against the reverse side. "I'm not ready to rot in the dark! Get off me--hands off! You cannot leave me here!"

The shouting recedes deeper down the subterranean shaft, swallowed by distance until the hallway settles into uneasy silence.

"What in the name of the frozen north was that?" Hadrik demands, staring at the bolted iron plate.

"It looks grim to outsiders, but our backs are against the stone," Breynan says. "An incurable blight runs rampant through Ollendor, and our scouts catch it on the surface. We don't know the vector, the source, or a remedy. The onset is always identical: three pink lesions erupt at the nape of the neck. Exactly seven days later, the fever breaks out and the disease turns violently contagious. From there, it's wracking coughs, delirium, and total organ failure. No one survives. Isolating the infected the instant the marks appear is the only way to keep the rot from wiping out the entire sanctuary."

"No," Daren says, his voice shaking with anger. "Barricading them in the dark to die is murder. There has to be a counter-weave. I am going in."

"Hold on, you have no authorization--" Breynan starts, but Daren shoulders past him.

The healer strides straight for the iron door, moving with a fierce urgency that ignores his bruised ribs and battered leg.

"I'll rein him in," I tell Breynan, jogging after him.

Before I can grab his arm, Daren halts before the sentry.

"Unbar the door," Daren demands. "I am examining the ward."

I pull up a pace behind Daren and watch the standoff.

"No entry," Desmond says, resting a hand on the bolt. "Especially for outsiders. Return to your bench until the king summons you. There is nothing for you here."

"You misunderstand," Daren says, stepping closer. "I am a master healer. Let me inspect the patients, and I can isolate the affliction."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Iknar, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Iknar)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Iknar
TEXT:

"I know your name, armored healer," Desmond says. "I know what you achieved on the eastern continent. But this blight bears no resemblance to the Iknar plague you cleansed. We had seasoned practitioners of our own, and none could decipher it. We buried two of our finest healers before accepting the truth: there is no remedy. I will not let you throw your life into the pit."

"So you abandon your own comrades to rot?" Daren says, fury bleeding into his words. "They are your people!"

"Do not lecture me on loss, swordsman," Desmond snaps, his voice vibrating with barely restrained rage. "I buried my wife in those catacombs last month, and my brother sits behind that iron plate waiting for his fever to spike. If I tell you it is impossible, it is impossible! You would only add another corpse to the ledger. No skill overcomes the inevitable."

"That does not excuse turning our backs," Daren says, his gaze hardening with fierce resolve. "Listen to me, Desmond. When I entered this tournament, I swore not to be swayed from the Magium. I told myself that claiming the source was the only path that mattered, because with that power, I could mend thousands in the time it takes to treat one broken bone on the road. But that promise means nothing if I walk past dying men today. By the time the tournament concludes, every soul behind that iron will be dust. I will not carry that cowardice to my grave."

Daren takes a step forward, planting both hands firmly on Desmond's shoulders and looking him dead in the eye.

"Desmond. Let me in. Let me try for your brother."

Desmond stares back, the hostility draining from his weathered face into weary contemplation.

He knocks Daren's hands away with a gruff scoff. "You are out of your mind, healer. A stark-raving fool--worse than that lunatic Kelrim."

Desmond turns to the door, unlocking the iron padlocks one by one before pulling the heavy slab ajar.

"Have it your way," Desmond mutters. "You have one week. If you show no results within seven days, you stay clear of this compound or join them permanently. You were warned."

"Understood," Daren says. "I will not squander the chance."

He glances over his shoulder at me.

"With me, Barry!"

choice(""Wait, that's not why I followed you here!"", B3-Ch02c-Lousy, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Lousy, v_b3_ch2_kelrim = 0)
choice(""Alright. Lead the way!"", B3-Ch02c-Cards, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Cards, v_b3_ch2_kelrim = 1)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Lousy
TEXT:

"Wait, that's not why I followed you here!"

"It's not?" Daren asks, pausing.

"I ran over to pull you back, but you were already through the door," I say. "If your mind is made up, all I can do is wish you luck."

"Ah," Daren says, his shoulders dipping with slight disappointment. "Well... thank you, then. I will try not to make a mess of things."

He turns and steps past the threshold. Desmond pulls the iron slab shut and locks the padlocks one by one. Seeing no reason to linger by the gate, I head back across the antechamber to where the others wait by the wall.

"Couldn't talk him out of it?" Kate asks as I take a seat.

"His heels were dug in," I say. "Nothing short of a stun bolt was stopping him."

"He'll pull through," Hadrik says with a shrug. "We've crawled out of tighter spots than a common fever. The lad will sort it out."

While Daren tends to the infected in the ward, the rest of us pass the time conferring with Breynan and Reidzek about the underground sanctuary and its uneasy coexistence with the city above. Just as the political landscape comes into focus, the heavy iron door clanks open.

Daren limps back into the antechamber, followed closely by a goblin dressed in white mage robes.

"A goblin?" Illuna asks, her eyes narrowing as the two approach.

"This is Kelrim," Daren says. "A goblin white mage who has volunteered in the quarantine ward for the past two weeks."

"Two weeks?" I ask. "Shouldn't that make him contagious by now?"

"Goblin physiology develops the sickness at half the rate," Daren explains. "He has roughly two weeks before the lesions turn infectious. He cast a temporary ward on me that shielded me from the disease, and he helped me diagnose its true nature."

"Daren partnering with a monster?" Hadrik's laugh rumbles. "Now there's a sight I never expected to see."

"The healer speaks the truth," Breynan says after inspecting the back of Daren's neck. "That ward held the rot at bay."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Strangers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Strangers)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Cards
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch2_reckless,1)
achievement("Reckless",v_ac_b3_ch2_reckless)
"Alright. Lead the way!" I say.

"Two lunatics for the price of one," Desmond mutters, shaking his gray head with grim disbelief.

"They don't call me 'Barry the Reckless' for nothing," I say. "See you on the other side."

I follow Daren past the threshold. Desmond heaves the heavy iron door shut behind us, the heavy clatter of iron deadbolts sealing us into the shaft.

We make our way down a narrow, damp stone corridor illuminated by a pair of sputtering pitch torches. At the far end stands a second iron door, sitting unlatched. Muffled voices filter through the seam, where the old man Jack is locked in a raw argument with other inmates.

"Pull yourself together, Jack," a tired voice snaps from beyond the timber. "You've done nothing but wail since they hauled you through the hatch. We had to drag you clear of the corridor so the antechamber wouldn't hear your carry-on. Try to make use of your remaining good days. Pull up a stool. Take an ale. We have cards. We share the same dark bunk now--no point making the dark darker."

The argument cuts short as Daren and I push the door open and step into the cavernous sick ward.

A draft smelling of stale vinegar, sweat, and dried chamomile washes over us. Near the entrance, Jack and three younger men sit around a scarred wooden trestle table littered with clay mugs and a deck of grease-stained, bent cards. Beyond them, rows of rusted iron cots stretch into the gloom, occupied by dozens of shivering, feverish patients coughing weakly into wool blankets or staring blankly at the rough bedrock ceiling. In the center aisle, a lone goblin in white mage robes moves between the beds, checking pulses and adjusting blankets, too absorbed in his rounds to notice our entrance.

"A goblin?" Daren stops dead in his tracks, staring. "A goblin is tending these people?"

The goblin halts at a nearby cot, lifting both hands to channel a healing matrix over a feverish patient.

Daren winces, his healer instincts instantly taking over. "No, no, no! That is not how you seat that weave! Here, let me assist."

Daren limps forward in a hurry, ignoring the pain in his side.

I close the distance behind him. Daren steadies the goblin's right wrist while pressing his left palm to the sick man's chest. Tracking the mana flow with my aura sense, I feel Daren guide the turbulent stream, smoothing out the jagged ripples until the clean white light settles evenly across the patient's torso. Daren steps back, releasing the goblin's arm.

"There," Daren says, his breath slightly short. "That stabilizes the flow. You should never force an advanced recovery matrix without locking the secondary channel first--the rebound will tear your own aura apart."

"My thanks for the instruction, kind stranger," the goblin says in remarkably fluent, cultured Common. "As you can see, I am merely a novice practitioner, but I offer what aid I can. I am Kelrim. A pleasure to make your acquaintance."

He extends a slender green hand. Daren shakes it after a brief, startled hesitation.

"Daren," he says. "And this is Barry. Desmond at the door mentioned your name--called you a fool. What brings you into an active quarantine ward? Were you locked in with the infected?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Healing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Healing)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Healing
TEXT:

"Oh, I wasn't sick when I first came here," Kelrim says, adjusting the hem of his white robe. "When I saw what was happening to these people, I could not turn away. As long as there was a chance to ease their suffering or uncover a cure, I had to take it."

"Why risk your life for humans?" Daren asks, genuinely perplexed. "You are a goblin. What makes their survival your concern?"

"I might ask the same of you, human named Daren," Kelrim replies evenly, his large amber eyes meeting Daren's without flinching. "Why do you care? You are an outsider, and these patients are strangers. Why risk everything for them? Because we are healers. It is in our nature to preserve life, regardless of race or species. Was that not your purpose when you took up the white mantle? To make a difference?"

"I..." Daren trails off, momentarily at a loss for words, looking down at his armored hands. "I suppose..."

Kelrim turns toward an adjacent cot, his eyes catching the bare skin at the base of Daren's skull.

"By the gods!" Kelrim gasps, nearly dropping his bundle of herbs. "Your neck--there are no marks! What are you doing in an active ward without the sickness? The spores will take root any second! Stand still--I will cast a ward!"

Kelrim raises his hands in a swift, practiced gesture. Bright white light envelopes Daren, a clean mana barrier wrapping tightly around his aura like a translucent second skin.

"And for your companion," Kelrim adds, immediately casting the identical white matrix over my chest. "Now leave, at once! This barrier degrades within an hour, and the channels can only sustain the weave once every few days. If you linger in this air, I cannot protect you!"

"Set that aside for a moment," Daren says, inspecting the glowing residue humming around his gauntlets. "The ward you just cast--the structure felt familiar."

"It is a modified derivation from the basic wards recorded in Advanced Theory of Magic, by Sage Azarius," Kelrim says.

"That treatise again..." Daren mutters. "Barry, you studied that volume. Which formulation is he referencing?"

"Judging by the hand posture and the layered dispersal of white light," I say, "it's an adaptation of the ward that isolates magical afflictions targeting the aura."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Grim, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Grim)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Grim
TEXT:

"Yes, that is it, exactly!" Kelrim says, nodding eagerly. "I have spent two weeks in this ward trying to unravel the blight, but this temporary barrier is the furthest I have progressed. Fortunately, goblin physiology slows the incubation. I still have roughly two weeks before my own lesions turn contagious, so I intend to use every hour hunting a remedy."

"If the pathogen attacks the aura directly," Daren says, "I might isolate the distortion with direct contact and focused sense. Let me examine him."

Daren rests his calloused palm on the sick man's sternum, shutting his eyes to trace the inner mana flow. Half a minute passes in complete silence, broken only by the raspy breathing of the patients. When Daren's eyes open, his face is grim as ash.

"Desmond was right," Daren breathes, pulling his hand back. "This IS impossible. The corruption isn't attacking the physical tissue—it's rotting the spirit link from within, inverting the aura's polarity. Standard counter-weaves can't touch it. Unless..."

Daren straightens, wincing as his bruised ribs protest the movement.

"Unless what?" Kelrim asks.

"There is one entity who possesses the sight to dissect this, though extracting her cooperation will be an ordeal," Daren says. "Follow me."

"Lead on," Kelrim nods.

We retrace our steps down the torchlit corridor and rap firmly on the heavy iron door.

Desmond unlocks the hatch and peers through the slit. "Given up already, healer?"

"Not yet," Daren says, leading us back into the antechamber where our companions wait near the wall.

"A goblin?" Illuna asks, her eyes narrowing as we approach.

"This is Kelrim," Daren says. "A goblin white mage who has volunteered in the quarantine ward for the past two weeks. He cast a temporary ward on Barry and me that shielded us from the disease, and he helped me diagnose its true nature."

"Daren partnering with a monster?" Hadrik's laugh rumbles, though his swollen eye squints with curious respect. "Now there's a sight I never expected to see."

"The healer speaks the truth," Breynan says after stepping forward and inspecting the clean skin at the nape of our necks. "That temporary ward held the rot at bay."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Strangers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Strangers)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Strangers
TEXT:

"So, if you already managed to figure out the nature of the sickness, then why did you come back here?" Kate asks, her arms crossed beneath her cloak.

"Because I cannot cure it alone," Daren says, his voice gravelly with fatigue. "The blight warps the patient's aura from within, but my magical perception is too coarse to map the corruption or weave a counter-spell. That is why I returned."

He turns toward Flower and Illuna. In the dim crystal light, Daren lowers himself slowly, the heavy steel of his grieves and knee-cops striking the cold flagstones with a dull, echoing clang. He goes down on one knee, rests his hands on his armored thigh, and bows his scarred forehead toward the stone.

"Arraka, I need your help," Daren says. The words are measured, but the effort behind them is palpable. "You are the only person I know who has a good enough magical sense to understand how this disease works. I realize how pathetic this is, but I have nobody else to turn to and if I don't do anything, all those people will die. Please... will you help me?"

A dead, heavy silence grips the chamber. No one moves. Kate stares at him in stunned stillness; Hadrik's hand freezes in his beard; Breynan and Reidzek look on in complete disbelief. For a proud knight of Daren's stature to bend his knee before the amulet is a surrender that costs him every shred of pride.

From the golden amulet resting against Flower's chest, a low, rasping chuckle breaks the quiet, swelling into a cruel, delighted hiss.

"What on earth is this?" Arraka's voice echoes off the seredium walls. "Is groveling before your enemies to rescue stray peasants your latest habit?"

She pauses for several seconds, letting Daren remain bowed in the dust before everyone.

"Hmm..." Arraka murmurs, savoring the moment. "You know what? I accept. I could not care less if those wretches rot where they lie, but if this drives you to hone your aura sight beyond that of a blind mule, it serves my purposes. Do not expect me to spoon-feed you the cure. You will weave the solution, not me. I will offer only vague hints to steer you in the right direction. And do not assume I will go easy on you because your life hangs in the balance. If you succumb to a petty fever, it merely proves you were never fit to face Selkram. Well, healer? Do you still want my counsel?"

"I do," Daren says without lifting his head.

"Then we have a deal," Arraka sneers. "Show me those sick patients of yours, and we can begin the dissection whenever you please."

Kelrim steps forward as Daren pushes himself back to his feet with a stiff grunt. "Daren, are you certain? I cannot cast the ward a second time. If you re-enter the ward now, you will catch the disease for good."

"I accepted that risk the moment I stepped past the sentry," Daren says, adjusting his sword belt. "Illuna, hand over the amulet."

"Hold," Breynan intervenes, raising a calloused hand. "Do not return to the ward just yet. The king of the underground should be arriving any minute now, and you will want to hear what he has to say. You will have ample time to attend to the sick once our council concludes."

"Very well," Daren exhales heavily, the weight of what he just did still hanging over him. "Let him make haste."

"Aye, this king takes his sweet time," Hadrik grumbles, shifting his stance. "Hopefully Albert didn't bump into any more old friends on the way, or we'll grow gray beards waiting."

"There is no harm in the delay," Reidzek says gently, seeking to ease the tension in the room. "It offers us a chance to converse. For instance, I imagine several of you have questions for our friend Kelrim here. It is not every day one encounters a goblin white mage within a human city."

choice(""I have to admit that I was wondering how Kelrim got to learn white magic."", B3-Ch02c-River, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-River, v_b3_ch2_question = 0)
choice(""Now that you mention it, I am a bit curious to know how a goblin ended up in a human city."", B3-Ch02c-River, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-River, v_b3_ch2_question = 1)
choice(""Actually, I can't say that I care all that much, to be honest."", B3-Ch02c-River, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-River, v_b3_ch2_question = 2)


ID: B3-Ch02c-River
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_question == 0) {
"I have to admit that I was wondering how Kelrim got to learn white magic."

"I learned primarily from books," Kelrim says. "Goblins rarely practice healing arts, so I lacked a formal instructor. The standard white mage primer provided a foundation, but I devoured every treatise I could scavenge. Over time, I began testing minor alterations to established matrices to observe the outcomes. Naturally, being an unrefined novice, many experiments collapsed."

"If you have successfully modified spell matrices without blowing your own channels apart, you are far from a novice," Illuna observes. "Most beginner practitioners cling rigidly to rote formulae and lack the courage to adjust a single node."

"Is that so?" Kelrim asks, blinking in surprise. "I assumed that was the standard path of study. In any case, I have a question of my own. You recently traveled the approach to Ollendor. Did you encounter three goblins on the road? They were due to reach the city days ago, and I left the weekly entry passphrase carved onto a river stone nearby, but I have received no word."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_question == 1) {
"Now that you mention it, I am a bit curious to know how a goblin ended up in a human city."

"It is a straightforward tale," Kelrim says. "Word reached our clan that Ollendor permitted goblins within its walls, so a companion and I traveled here to seek our fortunes. After settling in, my friend journeyed back to recruit two elders from our old camp. In the interim, circumstances unraveled, and I joined the resistance as you see."

He pauses briefly.

"Speaking of my companions... Did you happen to cross paths with three goblins on the road? They were due to arrive days ago, and I left the weekly entry passphrase carved onto a river stone nearby, but I have received no word."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_question == 2) {
"Actually, I can't say that I care all that much, to be honest."

"Understandable," Kelrim says quietly. "We are strangers, after all. Still, if I may trouble you with an inquiry, you traveled the approach to Ollendor recently."

"Of course," Daren says. "What is on your mind?"

"Did you encounter three goblins on your journey?" Kelrim asks. "They were due to reach the city days ago, and I left the weekly entry passphrase carved onto a river stone nearby, but I have received no word."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 1) {
"Those goblins you mentioned..." Daren's voice falters, the color draining completely from his face. "Were they equipped in scavenged leather armor and notched blades? Was one noticeably older than the other two?"

"Yes, exactly!" Kelrim says, leaning forward with sudden, eager hope. "You encountered them?"

"Yes, but..." Daren struggles for breath, his hands trembling at his sides. "We... killed them. Every one of them. We mistook them for Tyrath's vanguard... Kelrim, forgive us. We did not know... We simply..."

Kelrim stares at him in stunned disbelief, the words slowly penetrating his shock.

"My friends are... dead?" Kelrim whispers, his voice cracking. He gazes into empty air, as if the room and everyone in it had vanished.

His knees give out. He stumbles back a half-step and collapses heavily into a wooden chair, staring at the floor in hollow, shattered silence.

Beside me, Kate goes completely rigid. Her face turns to pale stone, her jaw clenched so tight the tendon in her neck stands out. Beneath the hem of her green cloak, her fists are white-knuckled, but her dark eyes remain cold, sharp, and locked unblinkingly on the floor. Hadrik looks down at his boots, unable to meet Kelrim's gaze.

"Kelrim..." Daren says softly, extending a hand before pulling it back, overwhelmed with shame. "I am so sorry..."

"No... do not apologize," Kelrim murmurs, his voice small and hollow in the cavern. "I do not blame you. They were goblins... Foreigners have no cause to hesitate before striking. In their place, another warband might have cut you down without a second thought... I understand how this land operates. But still... I need a moment..."

Kate does not speak. Her breath comes shallow and fast through her nose, her entire posture wound tight as an overwound spring, shutting out the room.

"Kate," I murmur in an undertone, stepping half a pace closer.

Her eyes flick to me, sharp as icicles. "I'm fine, Barry," she cuts in, her voice brittle as frozen glass. "Leave it."

Before I can answer, a sudden movement makes Breynan whirl around. He recoils a half-step in surprise, coming face to face with a man in his mid-fifties clad in fine chainmail beneath a charcoal gray mantle.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_password == 1 && v_b3_ch2_goblins == 0) {
"Did the carving on the stone read 'griffin beak' by any chance?" I ask.

"Technically, yes," Kelrim says. "Though the glyphs follow a substitution cipher known only to my companions. Decoded, the phrase becomes 'gargoyle wings.' How did you know what was carved onto the stone?"

"Melindra pocketed it for her collection," I say.

"What?" Melindra snaps, crossing her arms. "There was no writing on any of my stones!"

"As you can see, she took it without reading it," I say.

"I said the surface was completely clean!" Melindra pouts. "Stop talking over me!"

"Then without the passphrase, my companions remain stranded outside the gates," Kelrim says, watching her grumble.

"They are definitely outside," I say. "Three goblins hailed us near the forest road, but we ignored them and kept moving."

"I see," Kelrim nods. "I will venture out and locate them once our business concludes."

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_password == 0 && v_b3_ch2_goblins == 0) {
"We crossed paths with some goblins on the road," I say. "They tried to flag us down regarding the city password, but we ignored them and kept moving."

"Those were likely my companions," Kelrim says. "I will search for them outside the walls once our business here concludes."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 2) {
"We ran into three goblins near the outer woods," I say. "We had a brief exchange about that missing stone. They couldn't locate it by the bank and accused us of taking it. They're likely still camped in the tree line outside the village."

"I see," Kelrim says. "I will venture out and find them as soon as I can."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3) {
"We crossed paths with them, actually," I say. "We had pocketed the stone by mistake, so we traded the decrypted phrase in exchange for the gatekeeper's three-wink trick. Your friends should be safely inside the city by now."

"In that case, they are likely waiting at our safehouse," Kelrim says, visibly relieved. "I will join them as soon as possible."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4) {
"We ran into them on the road," I say. "We had the stone by mistake, so we traded the phrase for the three-wink gate trick. Your friends slipped past the gate just ahead of us and are likely roaming the upper wards right now."

"In that case, they are likely waiting at our safehouse," Kelrim says, visibly relieved. "I will join them as soon as possible."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 0 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 2 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4) {
A sudden movement makes Breynan whirl around. He recoils a half-step in surprise, coming face to face with a man in his mid-fifties clad in fine chainmail beneath a charcoal gray mantle.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02c-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02c-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch02c-Ending
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 1) {
"My king!" Breynan gasps, dropping into a deep bow.

"No need to disrupt your conversation on my account," the newcomer says, holding up a gloved hand as we turn toward him. "Please, finish what you were discussing. I can wait."

As we stand in tense silence, Kate draws her green cloak tighter across her shoulders, her jaw tight as she forces her breathing into a calm, steady rhythm.

"Finished, then?" the man in the mantle asks, arching an eyebrow. "Good. In that case, accept my apologies for the delay. I was occupied with a sensitive council that could not be set aside, preventing me from receiving you upon arrival. As the fault is mine, I came to escort you personally."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins != 1) {
"My king!" Breynan gasps, dropping into a deep bow.

"No need to disrupt your conversation on my account," the newcomer says, holding up a gloved hand as we turn toward him. "Please, finish what you were discussing. I can wait."

We trade uneasy glances, meeting the newcomer's assessing stare in silence.

"Finished, then?" the man in the mantle asks, arching an eyebrow. "Good. In that case, accept my apologies for the delay. I was occupied with a sensitive council that could not be set aside, preventing me from receiving you upon arrival. As the fault is mine, I came to escort you personally."

}
"You're the king of the underground?" Daren asks.

"In the flesh," the man in the gray mantle says.

"So where are you taking us?" Hadrik asks.

"To my throne room, naturally," the king of the underground says. "Follow me."

He turns and strides toward the far archway, pausing mid-step to glance back over his shoulder.

"I do hope your calendar is clear for the evening," he adds smoothly. "We have a great deal to discuss."

